<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anna Amalia of Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1739–1807</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anna Amalia of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a> was Duchess of Saxe-Weimar-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisenach">Eisenach</a> and regent for her son Charles Augustus from 1758 to 1775. A niece of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a> and a composer herself, she invited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christoph-martin-wieland">Christoph Martin Wieland</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> as tutor and made her court a centre of literature and music. Her library, the Duchess Anna Amalia Library, is part of the Classical Weimar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anna-amalia-of-brunswick-wolfenbuttel.jpg" alt="Anna Amalia of Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel" width="320" height="437" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johann Ernst Heinsius / After Johann Georg Ziesenis · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Weimar_Anna_Amalia_Bibliothek@Anna_Amalie_von_Sachsen-Weimar_(1).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
